{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,2,21]],"date-time":"2025-02-21T21:37:53Z","timestamp":1740173873009,"version":"3.37.3"},"reference-count":108,"publisher":"The Royal Society","issue":"10","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2020,10,1]],"date-time":"2020-10-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1601510400000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2020,10,1]],"date-time":"2020-10-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1601510400000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100007040","name":"Singapore University of Technology and Design","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","award":["PIE-SGP-AI-2020-01"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["PIE-SGP-AI-2020-01"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100007040","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"crossref"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100001381","name":"National Research Foundation Singapore","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","award":["NRF 2018 Fellowship NRF-NRFF2018-07","NRF2019-NRF-ANR095 ALIAS grant"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["NRF 2018 Fellowship NRF-NRFF2018-07","NRF2019-NRF-ANR095 ALIAS grant"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100001381","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"crossref"}]},{"name":"MOE AcRF Tier 2 Grant","award":["2016-T2-1-170"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["2016-T2-1-170"]}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":["royalsocietypublishing.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["R. Soc. open sci."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,10]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Black\u2013Scholes (BS) is a remarkable quotation model for European option pricing in financial markets. Option prices are calculated using an analytical formula whose main inputs are strike (at which price to exercise) and volatility. The BS framework assumes that volatility remains constant across all strikes; however, in practice, it varies. How do traders come to learn these parameters? We introduce natural agent-based models, in which traders update their beliefs about the true implied volatility based on the opinions of other agents. We prove exponentially fast convergence of these opinion dynamics, using techniques from control theory and leader-follower models, thus providing a resolution between theory and market practices. We allow for two different models, one with feedback and one with an unknown leader.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1098\/rsos.201188","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2020,10,21]],"date-time":"2020-10-21T07:05:48Z","timestamp":1603263948000},"page":"201188","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1098\/crossmark-policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":2,"title":["Learning agents in Black\u2013Scholes financial markets"],"prefix":"10.1098","volume":"7","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2264-2595","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Tushar","family":"Vaidya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore University of Technology and Design, 8 Somapah Road, Singapore 487372, Singapore"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8887-1390","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Carlos","family":"Murguia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Eindhoven University of Technology, Department of Mechanical Engineering, 5612 AZ Eindhoven, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6236-3566","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Georgios","family":"Piliouras","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore University of Technology and Design, 8 Somapah Road, Singapore 487372, Singapore"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"175","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2020,10,21]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_3_7_2_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1080\/14697688.2012.704692"},{"key":"e_1_